WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.712 --> 00:00:08.020
NARRATOR: Lining a
cooking or baking pan

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.810
with culinary parchment
paper instantly

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.050
makes cleanup a whole lot
easier because nothing

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.040
sticks to the pan.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.180
With parchment paper, there's
no need to grease the pan

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.740
with messy butter or oil.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.890
This culinary
parchment paper comes

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.325
in a standard sheet roll or an
assortment of specialty shapes.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.415
It's heat resistant to a
temperature of 450 degrees.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.370
Making paper begins with pulp,
which is pulverized wood.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.730
This parchment-paper
manufacturer

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:53.195
uses eucalyptus because
it's a sustainable resource.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.990
They mix the pulp with water
in a huge industrial mixer.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.240
This special recipe
of eucalyptus fibers

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.740
produces tan-colored paper.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.270
Once the consistency
is just right,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.760
they pump the soggy
pulp out of the blender

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.410
and into a machine
that spreads it

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.820
evenly across a metal
mesh belt. The water

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.620
drains through the
openings in the mesh.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.860
By the time the pulp
rolls into the first term

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.755
of the next machine, it is
formed into a sheet of paper.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.610
Sprayers rehydrate what's
trimmed off the edges

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.600
so it can be recycled.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.385
The paper travels through a
long line of heated rollers.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.045
They squeeze out the remaining
water and dry the paper.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.320
A high-resolution scanner
looks for imperfections

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.025
as the paper exits the machine.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:59.810
Workers unwind the bolt and
conduct a visual inspection

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.229
against backlighting.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.009
Next, the bolt
undergoes the first

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:12.150
of two critical operations which
transforms this regular paper

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.040
into culinary parchment paper.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.640
First, the sheet passes
through two baths

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.500
in a proprietary
water-based solution.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.680
This eliminates air
pockets and renders

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.030
the paper heat resistant.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.440
After a series of
rinses, another long line

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.780
of heated rollers squeezes
out the rinse water

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:37.830
and dries the paper.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.760
The next station
sprays both sides

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.930
of the sheet with
food-grade silicon,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.720
then bakes on the coating
in a gas-fired dryer.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.450
Silicon withstands heat
up to 1,500 degrees.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.510
So even at the highest
cooking temperatures,

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.580
there will be a barrier
that prevents food

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.645
from sticking to the paper.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.800
Silicone also repels water.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.300
This is the paper
before the treatments

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.060
make it heat resistant,
nonstick, and waterproof.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.980
And here's how
the paper performs

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.678
after those treatments.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.320
Because it's
waterproof, you can wet

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.450
the paper to make it
malleable enough to line

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.680
a pan of any shape.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.150
The factory cuts the
bolts to consumer widths,

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.910
then shrink wraps and ships
them to factories that supply

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.440
restaurants and supermarkets.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.290
Here, machines unwind
the bolts, then

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.420
wind specific lengths
of parchment paper

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.440
onto cardboard tubes.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.255
Winding a 165 foot roll
takes just three seconds.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.260
The factory also applies
its logo to the paper

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.490
at regular intervals.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.050
However, that operation
is top secret.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.610
The automated packaging
equipment tapes down the edge

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.740
to prevent unraveling,
then places and seals

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.120
the roll in a cardboard box.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.950
The machine stamps the
box with a lot code.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.350
So if a problem
arises, the factory

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.420
can trace the roll
back to the large bolt

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.095
from which it originated.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.170
This parchment paper, cardboard
tube, and box are all made

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.820
from sustainable resources.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.010
The box edge is rigid
enough to tear off a sheet,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.100
so there's no need
for an unrecyclable

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.120
metal cutting edge.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.070
You can throw the used parchment
paper in your compost bin

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.260
or rinse off the
food remnants and put

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:04.270
it, along with the box and
tube, in your recycling bin.

